Between 1990 and 1996, white-male composite wealth rose from $88,163 to $131,792 (Δ = $43,630, +49.5%) and the median changed from $15,601 to $8,900 (-43.0%), while for white females the mean moved from $99,605 to $128,837 (Δ = $29,232, +29.3%) and the median from $20,100 to $8,825 (-56.1%) — both 1990 means already sat above their 1985 baselines of $30,167 (white-male) and $33,451 (white-female).
Inequality among white males moved slightly: the Gini went from 0.79202 to 0.79747 (absolute Δ = +0.00545), the 90/50 percentile ratio from 13.7907 to 37.4876 (Δ = +23.6969), and the 90/10 ratio from inf to inf on the full, negatives-included distribution.
Inequality among white females also moved: Gini from 0.77559 to 0.78160 (Δ = +0.00601), 90/50 ratio from 12.0672 to 37.5921 (Δ = +25.5249), and 90/10 from inf to inf (reported as inf when p10 ≤ 0: white females had 1614 observations ≤ 0 in 1996 vs 917 in 1990).
In both groups the mean rose by tens of thousands of dollars while the median moved far less and the Gini change is small in absolute terms, so the dominant signal between 1990 and 1996 is a level shift with modestly widening top-tail inequality but stable overall dispersion; the change is descriptive, not causal — we are not attributing it to any of the unresolved five wealth components, only documenting the distribution's motion.
Treatment of negatives: zero and negative composite-wealth observations are retained in every stat (no shift, no drops, no winsorization); the Gini uses the canonical sorted-mean form G = (2·Σ i·x_(i) − (n+1)·Σ x_(i)) / (n·Σ x_(i)), which equals Σ (2i−n−1)x_(i) / (n²·μ) and handles negative values natively when the cell's μ > 0 (μ > 0 for all four reported cells).
